# libpdx-unicode-tables -- caps.decl
#
# Wave: R131 (semantic shell -- Unicode substrate umbrella,
# paideia-os#2480). libpdx-unicode-tables is a USER-SPACE shared
# library providing pure-leaf UTF-8 / UTF-16 / grapheme-cluster /
# normalization primitives for the shell (pds), the lexer, and every
# other ring-3 tool that walks text. It performs no `sys_svc_lookup`,
# binds no ambient service, and holds no cap of its own: every entry
# point is a pure leaf operating on caller-supplied raw pointers.
#
# This file therefore declares the ABSENCE of required capabilities
# at the ENTRY-POINT floor -- a downstream tool linking this library
# does not need to grant anything beyond its own data-buffer read /
# write capability. Every primitive here is `effects: {mem},
# capabilities: {}` or `effects: {}, capabilities: {}` (lib.pdx's
# feature-level accessor is pure).
#
# consumed_kinds: none at M1 or M2.
#
#   Future milestones (deferred -- see src/lib.pdx):
#     M3 streaming decoder (opaque-handle convention, no new KIND)
#     M4 utf16 bridge (no new KIND)
#     M5 stream validators (no new KIND)
#     M6 normalization (reads UCD-pinned embedded tables)
#
# None of M2..M6 is expected to introduce a KIND surface this library
# itself invokes against; the UCD tables are compiled IN (as `[u8; N]`
# bindings), not fetched through a cap. If a future performance pass
# promotes the UCD tables to a shared, memory-mapped KIND_UCD_TABLE
# surface for inter-process sharing, this file gets amended then.
#
# declares_output_schemas: none. This library emits no semantic-pipe
# records; its only user-visible outputs are the four utf8 primitives'
# return values (packed codepoint + bytes_consumed, or the UTF8_*
# error band in SECTION 2 of src/utf8.pdx). A downstream consumer
# (e.g. the shell's lexer) that chooses to log a decode fingerprint
# does so via its own `klog_s1_*` wrapper on its own schema -- this
# library's primitives neither call klog nor know about one.
#
# ---- Error band 0xFFFFE700..0xFFFFE70F (M1, utf8) --------------------
#
# Fresh 16-wide aligned user-space error-code slice. First five slots
# used at M1 (UTF8_BAD_UTF8 .. UTF8_DST_TOO_SMALL, src/utf8.pdx
# SECTION 2); slots 5..F reserved for later utf8 error kinds (e.g.
# STATE_INVALID when the streaming decoder lands at M3). Disjoint from:
#
#   * libpdx-elevate   : 0xFFFFE5E0..EF, 0xFFFFEA00..7F
#   * libpdx-volume    : internal 0x0100-band taxonomy (lpv_errors.pdx)
#                        plus the kernel-side PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER
#                        sentinel at 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA
#   * paideia-os shell : 0xFFFFECD/ECE/ED8..EDF (user-space ret bands)
#   * paideia-os mv    : 0xFFFFEBC/EBD
#   * kernel drivers   : 0xFFFFFC_ / FFFFFD_ clusters (disjoint cluster)
#
# Grep confirmation at M1 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0xFFFFE70[0-9A-F]' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no hits anywhere.
#
# ---- Error band 0xFFFFE710..0xFFFFE71F (M2, grapheme) ----------------
#
# Second 16-wide aligned user-space error-code slice taken by this
# library (R131.M2, paideia-os#2613). First slot used:
#
#   GC_INVALID_UTF8  : 0xFFFFE710  (returned by _gc_count on any
#                                   malformed sub-sequence, matching
#                                   utf8_count_cp's wc -m refusal)
#
# Slots 1..F are reserved for later grapheme-layer error kinds (e.g.
# GC_STATE_INVALID if a streaming grapheme-iter surface lands with a
# resume path). The grapheme STATUS codes (0=OK, 1=EOF, 2=INVALID_UTF8)
# live INSIDE the packed return of _gc_next and are NOT drawn from
# this band -- they do not conflict with any user-space ret band
# because they occupy the low 32 bits of a 64-bit packed value whose
# high 32 bits are the next_cursor.
#
# Grep confirmation at M2 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0xFFFFE71[0-9A-F]' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no hits anywhere outside this file + grapheme.pdx.
#
# ---- Error band 0xFFFFE720..0xFFFFE72F (M3, normalize) ---------------
#
# Third 16-wide aligned user-space error-code slice taken by this
# library (R131.M3, paideia-os#2614). Two slots used:
#
#   NORM_INVALID_UTF8_CODE : 0xFFFFE720  (internal sentinel; the
#                                         public API returns
#                                         status=2 in the packed
#                                         result rather than the
#                                         raw band value, matching
#                                         the _gc_next-style packed
#                                         convention)
#   NORM_OUT_TOO_SMALL_CODE: 0xFFFFE721  (internal sentinel; the
#                                         public API returns
#                                         status=1 in the packed
#                                         result rather than the
#                                         raw band value)
#
# Slots 2..F are reserved for later normalize-layer error kinds
# (SCRATCH_OVERFLOW once that becomes its own distinct status at
# M3.1, TABLE_VERSION_MISMATCH when the UCD-pinned tables become
# run-time-swappable at M3.2). The normalize STATUS codes
# (0=OK, 1=OUT_TOO_SMALL, 2=INVALID_UTF8) live INSIDE the packed
# return of _norm_nf{c,d,kc,kd} and are not drawn from this band.
#
# The internal band values (0xFFFFE720, 0xFFFFE721) are documented
# constants but the public API never returns them raw: they are
# visible only through the module's `pub let NORM_INVALID_UTF8_CODE`
# and `NORM_OUT_TOO_SMALL_CODE` for consumers wiring their own
# downstream error-propagation paths that need to distinguish the
# library's two internal sentinels.
#
# Grep confirmation at M3 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0xFFFFE72[0-9A-F]' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no hits anywhere outside this file + normalize.pdx.
